<h1>Your First Tattoo: A Complete, Calm Walkthrough</h1>
<%- include('../partials/byline') %>
<p class="muted">Your first tattoo, in the order it actually happens — the
checks, the choosing, the chair, and the healing.</p>

<p>A first tattoo is really three projects wearing one coat. There is the
decision: what goes on you, and whether it should go on you yet. There is the
transaction: finding an artist whose work heals well, agreeing a design, a
size, a placement, and a price. And there is the healing: two to four weeks
where the result is mostly in your hands. People who regret tattoos usually
skipped one of the three — they fell for a design in an afternoon, or chose
the artist by whoever had a free chair, or healed it carelessly on a beach
holiday. None of the three projects is hard. All three reward going in the
right order, which is what this page does. It is general information from
working artists and shops, not medical advice; where your own artist's
instructions differ from anything here, your artist wins — they saw your
skin.</p>

<h2>First, the gate: should you be tattooed at all, yet?</h2>
<p>Start with the unglamorous checks, because they are the ones that can turn
a good day bad. You must be an adult — reputable shops tattoo nobody under
18 and will ask for ID; a shop that waves that away is telling you how it
treats every other rule. Your skin at the spot matters: eczema or psoriasis
flaring there, a fresh scar, a sunburn, a rash, or broken skin are all
reasons to wait until the skin is calm and whole, because tattooing is
controlled injury and injured skin heals badly. If you have a history of
keloid scarring — raised, spreading scars — say so at the consultation; some
skin answers any wound by overbuilding scar tissue, and a good artist will
talk honestly about a small test or a different plan. If you take
blood-thinning medication, have a condition that affects your immune system,
or are pregnant, that is a conversation for your artist and, where you are
unsure, your doctor, before the appointment — not a surprise on the day.
None of this is about scaring you off. It is the same list an artist runs
through mentally in the first minute of meeting you, and arriving with the
answers makes you the kind of client artists do their best work for.</p>

<h2>Sit with the idea before you buy it</h2>
<p>The classic test: put the design where you will see it every day — a print
taped to the bathroom mirror, your phone's lock screen — and live with it
for two weeks. Wanting it on day fourteen, in the morning, in a bad mood, is
a different signal from wanting it on day one. Meaning helps a design last,
but it is not required; "I love how this looks" has carried tattoos happily
for sixty years. What reliably sours is the midnight category: a partner's
name from a new relationship, a joke that needs explaining, a lyric you
cannot stop singing this month. Time is the filter for all of them, and time
is free. If the idea survives two weeks of ordinary days, it has earned the
next step.</p>

<h2>Choose the artist like it matters — because it is most of the result</h2>
<p>The design is the plan; the artist is the building. Judge artists by
healed work: photographs of tattoos months or years old, settled into skin,
not the glossy shot taken under a ring light the minute the needle stopped.
Fresh tattoos always look sharp. Healed work shows you the truth about line
quality — whether the lines healed smooth and even, or wobbly, blown out
(fuzzy, where ink spread under the skin), or patchy. Look for an artist
whose portfolio is thick in the style you want; a brilliant traditional
artist is not automatically the right hand for a soft fine-line piece. Then
check the shop the way you would check a kitchen: needles should be
single-use and opened in front of you, surfaces barrier-wrapped, hands
gloved, and the whole setup should feel boring in its cleanliness. Finally,
the walk-away test. If an artist pressures you to decide now, refuses your
questions, or agrees to copy another artist's custom design line for line,
leave — politely, and for good. There are more artists than appointments,
and the right one will still be there next week.</p>

<h2>The consultation and the honest price</h2>
<p>Bring three things to the consultation: references (pictures of the
direction, not a design to steal), the placement, and the size in inches —
trace it on the skin with your hand so it is real. Expect to leave a
deposit; it reserves your appointment and usually counts toward the total
price, but ask, because shops differ. Ask whether the piece is priced by the
hour or as a flat piece price, and what the drawing process looks like —
when you see the design, and what happens if the first drawing misses. On
tipping: it is customary and appreciated, not mandatory; clients commonly
tip like they would in a restaurant when the work and the experience were
good, and artists remember clients who treat the chair well. One firm rule:
do not bargain-hunt a permanent purchase. The difference between a fair price
and a cheap one is small money; the difference in the mirror is forever, and
a cover-up or laser removal costs several times what the tattoo would have.</p>

<h2>The day of the appointment</h2>
<p>Sleep properly the night before. Eat a real meal within a couple of hours
of the appointment — tattooing on an empty stomach is how people faint, and
artists would always rather you ate. Drink water. Skip alcohol the night
before and the day itself: it thins the blood, which makes the artist's job
harder and the healing messier. Do not arrive sunburned on the spot, and do
not shave it aggressively yourself; your artist will prep the skin. Wear
clothes that give access to the area and that you will not mourn if ink
touches them, because it might. Bring your ID, your deposit balance, and
something to listen to if it is a long sit. If you have bought a design from
this gallery, bring both files — the linework for the stencil and the color
artwork as the reference.</p>

<h2>In the chair: stencil first, then the needle</h2>
<p>The session starts with the stencil: your design, transferred onto the
skin in purple outline. This is your last free checkpoint — use it properly.
Stand up. Look at it straight, sitting, standing, and in a mirror. Twist the
limb the way it moves in life. If it is lettering, read every single letter
out loud, slowly; a misspelling caught at the stencil costs nothing, and
caught at home it costs everything. If the placement sits wrong, say so —
artists re-stencil routinely and would rather move it three times than
tattoo a position you are politely doubting. When the needle starts, expect
a scratch that keeps going: sharp and attention-getting on bony or
thin-skinned ground, a duller buzz on padded areas, easing as your body
settles into it. Breathe out slowly as lines start; it genuinely helps.
Breaks are normal — ask for one before you need one. And speak up the moment
you feel faint, hot, or strange; artists would far rather pause for five
minutes than catch a falling client. One modern warning: if you plan to use
a numbing cream, tell your artist before the appointment, never surprise
them with it on the day — some artists will not work over it because it
changes how the skin takes ink.</p>

<h2>Afterward: healing is your half of the artwork</h2>
<p>When you stand up, the artist's half is done and yours has started. A
tattoo is a shallow wound with ink in it; the next two to four weeks decide
how it looks for the next forty years. Keep it clean, lightly moisturized,
and left alone. Expect the peel-and-itch phase around the end of the first
week, and do not pick at it — picking is the single most common cause of
patchy spots. The full day-by-day routine, the product choices, and the
warning signs that mean a doctor rather than more ointment are all in the
<a href="/guides/aftercare">complete aftercare guide</a>; read it before your
appointment, not after. Plan around the healing: no soaking, swimming, or
deliberate sun on the piece for the first fortnight, so book the tattoo
before the beach holiday, not during it.</p>

<h2>First-timer mistakes, collected</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Too small for the detail.</strong> A portrait the size of a coin
will not stay a portrait. Detail needs inches; if the artist says bigger,
believe them.</li>
<li><strong>Copying a placement from a photograph.</strong> A design that
sits beautifully on someone else's shoulder sits differently on yours —
bodies differ, and stencils are checked on your skin, not in a picture.</li>
<li><strong>Choosing in the lobby.</strong> Flash walls are for inspiration;
a design decided in four minutes under appointment pressure is how regret
starts. Sit with it first.</li>
<li><strong>Healing it like a dare.</strong> Tight gym clothes over a fresh
piece, a pool on day three, a sunburn on week one — the tattoo keeps the
receipts.</li>
<li><strong>Going quiet in the chair.</strong> Not asking for a break, not
mentioning the stencil looks off, not saying you feel faint. Artists are
professionals; speaking up is part of the appointment, not an interruption
of it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>The health gate run through — skin calm, questions answered, ID ready</li>
<li>The idea survived two ordinary weeks of looking at it</li>
<li>Design and style chosen, with their aging understood</li>
<li>The artist judged on healed work, in the right style, in a clean shop</li>
<li>Consultation done: size in inches, placement, price, deposit terms clear</li>
<li>Slept, fed, hydrated, sober; clothes with access; both files if you bought a design</li>
<li>Aftercare guide read before the appointment</li>
</ul>
